@startuml
!include style.iuml
title Native simulation · one clock, completed interrupt handoff
caption Linux functional verification; this does not model Cortex-M execution time.
footer Source: Session.cpp; simulation/Client.cpp; ThreadX Linux scheduler hook; DisplayHost.cpp.
autonumber
box "Native simulator process" #EDF4FE
participant "Session / Machine" as P
end box
boundary "Shared-memory\nIRQ sequence + idle epoch" as Q #FFF5DF
box "Controller process" #EAF7F4
participant "Interrupt thread" as I
participant "ThreadX scheduler" as S
participant "Homing worker" as H
participant "GUI / other workers" as G
end box

P -> P : advance to next event only when prior work is idle
P -> Q : publish reference input, IRQ flags and sequence N
Q -> I : eventfd wake
I -> I : reference callback latches activation
I -> S : notify homing worker
I -> Q : acknowledge IRQ N
note over P,Q
  IRQ acknowledgement does not authorize more virtual time.
  The notified homing worker must get a chance to run.
end note
I -> S : leave interrupt context
S -> H : dispatch ready worker in priority order
H -> Q : HAL transaction: stop STEP; hold virtual time
Q -> P : process register requests
P --> H : output stopped, exact count available
H -> S : wait for debounce / next event
S -> G : dispatch remaining runnable work
G -> S : suspend until notification / simulated deadline
S -> Q : no runnable threads, no ISR: idle_epoch = N + 1
Q --> P : matching IRQ acknowledgement and idle epoch
P -> P : next event may advance virtual time

note over P,G #FFF5DF
  ThreadX ticks, STEP, feedback and display VSYNC share virtual time.
  SDL input pumping and native peer watchdogs remain on wall time.
  Slow host computation stretches wall duration instead of starving a worker
  behind repeated simulated timer interrupts. GIGA firmware is unchanged.
end note
@enduml
